 of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{3}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables.\n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we only need to normalize them. This gives the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533557 17592186045585 true] [17592186408055 1062 #uuid "e2882521-8d21-5a7b-8813-60683b9344e3" 13194139533557 17592186045585 true] [17592186408055 1063 #edb/ref 17592186408052 13194139533557 17592186045585 true] [17592186408056 1058 #edb/ref 17592186408057 13194139533557 17592186045585 true] [17592186408056 1062 #uuid "e4884cf0-a1fa-595d-912e-314992ac2509" 13194139533557 17592186045585 true] [17592186408056 1063 #edb/ref 17592186408053 13194139533557 17592186045585 true] [17592186408057 1058 #edb/ref 17592186408055 13194139533557 17592186045585 true] [17592186408057 1062 #uuid "e6c10cae-3311-5de0-80be-f4f348e2b025" 13194139533557 17592186045585 true] [17592186408057 1063 #edb/ref 17592186408054 13194139533557 17592186045585 true]] :edb/tempids {"ac